"""Additional page-specific analysis and diagram specifications.

The enrichment is instructional. None of the figures shows observed execution,
measured performance, a live UI, or a provider-specific training assignment.
"""

# Every pair adds distinct explanatory content to the corresponding numbered page.
EXPANSIONS = [
("The greeting has a deliberately narrow input and output contract. Its input is a fixed text value chosen by the author; its output is the same visible text followed by the normal line ending. There is no user-supplied name, file path, database state or network connection to consider. Keeping the contract small lets a reader trace every stage without hiding a dependency behind the word 'simple'.",
 "A useful reading exercise is to underline the characters in the source that define structure and circle those that belong to the message. The structure consists of the function name, brackets and quotation delimiters; the message consists of the thirteen characters of the greeting. This distinction guides the later diagrams and prevents source notation from being mistaken for output."),
("For a first exercise, success is not defined by visual excitement but by an exact, predictable relationship between instruction and result. If the learner can explain why punctuation appears, why the source quotes do not appear and why the next prompt begins on another line, the example has done more than demonstrate typing skill. It has introduced a small specification that can be checked.",
 "Conversely, a displayed greeting cannot tell us whether a source file was saved in the right place, whether other instructions are correct or whether a larger program is reliable. An educator should assess understanding through explanation and comparison of variants, not by assuming that one printed sentence proves competence in unrelated topics."),
("Evidence has different strengths. A certificate supports the statement that the named internship was completed. It does not identify every exercise performed during that internship. A report may use public Python documentation to explain general language behaviour, but it must not convert that explanation into a statement that an employer assigned or assessed this exact example.",
 "The two student-provided documents also leave the exact internship start date unspecified. The offer's date is a document-issue date, not a verified commencement date. Accordingly, this study leaves the training dates as documented and asks the student to obtain confirmation if the department needs a four-to-six-week date range."),
("The objectives can be made assessable by attaching a question to each one. For syntax, can the reader name every role in the illustrative call? For output, can the reader write the expected thirteen-character text? For diagnosis, can the reader separate a missing-file problem from an unmatched quote? For evidence, can the reader say which statements are predictions rather than observations?",
 "These questions define the standard by which the study should be judged. They do not require a working prototype. A later practical exercise would add an observed transcript and would verify whether the predicted line matches a real run. Until then the present report stays at the level of reasoning and planning."),
("A boundary statement is useful because broad words such as 'project' can imply more work than the source warrants. In this report, project means a structured explanatory study, not a deliverable application. The scope includes language rules that directly affect the greeting and excludes claims about performance or business value that cannot be inferred from one output call.",
 "Even within this boundary there are useful distinctions: a print call is not a return statement, a visible line is not a saved file and a string's character count is not an execution benchmark. These distinctions are treated as legitimate learning outcomes rather than as separate applications built during training."),
("The overview of the organisation is deliberately source-limited. A public-facing brand in a certificate and a legal name in an offer may both appear in one training record; this report merely records those names as printed. It does not infer the organisation's internal technology stack, teaching schedule, employee roles or confidential datasets.",
 "A suitable internship report should be specific about what evidence actually says and cautious about what it does not. That principle matters especially for a basic topic: there is no need to invent an employer problem statement or pretend that an introductory output example changed an organisational workflow."),
("Python's built-in print function is part of the normal language environment and accepts values to display. It is different from the English instruction 'print on paper'. In programming discussion, print normally means write formatted text to a stream; a physical printer is not required. This terminological distinction helps beginners interpret the example without expecting a document to emerge from a printer.",
 "Python 3 is the assumed language family throughout. Older descriptions sometimes show print without parentheses; copying that form into a Python 3 lesson may cause confusion. Naming the assumed version next to the example keeps the discussion internally consistent, while avoiding an unsupported assertion about which interpreter was used during the internship."),
("Read the source left to right. The identifier print chooses an operation. The opening parenthesis starts the call's argument list. The opening quote begins a string literal, and the closing quote ends it. Finally the closing parenthesis finishes the call. The thirteen internal characters form the argument's text value.",
 "This order is also a manual correction technique. If an intended greeting is absent, check the paired delimiters before changing the message. If the program is valid but the visible line differs, inspect the characters inside the quotes instead. Separating structural checks from content checks makes the diagnosis shorter and more precise."),
("The same physical line can contain both human-readable words and characters with a formal syntactic role. The Python processor does not guess a programmer's intentions from the phrase 'Hello, World!'; it follows a grammar that recognises a call and a literal. A document that teaches the example should preserve both levels of meaning.",
 "A beginner may copy a line from a formatted document containing curly quotation marks rather than ordinary Python quote delimiters. In this report, decorative quotation marks in prose are typography; the source-like expression uses straight quotation marks. This is one reason to review the actual source text carefully in a future practical exercise."),
("Functions can accept zero, one or several arguments depending on their definition. For print, zero positional values still allow an output operation that ends a line; one string displays its text; several values introduce the separator rule. These three cases show why the argument list matters even when the function name stays unchanged.",
 "For this report, the single-string form is the reference. It puts the comma and space inside one value and removes the need to reason about inter-argument spacing. The multiple-argument variant is discussed to show a contrast, not because it is required for the simplest output task."),
("A literal is a way to write a value directly in source. The choice of surrounding single or double quotes need not change the value, provided each form is written correctly. But an apostrophe inside a single-quoted literal, or an unmatched delimiter of either kind, introduces a different parsing question that the basic greeting avoids.",
 "Text inside the literal should be treated as data, not as a Python command. If the word World is misspelled, Python will not repair it; a valid source line can still display the wrong greeting. Correct syntax and correct intended content are two independent checks."),
("The punctuation in the reference greeting is part of the value. Removing the comma shortens the visible text by one character, while replacing the exclamation mark changes one character without changing the length. Thus checking length alone is insufficient to confirm exact output.",
 "A careful specification may compare both length and content. For instance, two candidate strings can each contain thirteen characters yet disagree at one position. This is why an exact-output exercise should name the expected sequence rather than merely say 'print a sentence'."),
("Consider a pair of quotation marks around a phrase in an essay. Those marks may be displayed because they are part of the essay's text. In the source example, by contrast, the outer marks are a notation for constructing a string and are consumed as delimiters. A reader must first determine whether marks are syntax or data.",
 "The reasoning is not restricted to quotation marks. Parentheses and the function identifier likewise belong to the instruction, not to the greeting value. A diagram showing the two layers makes it harder to mistake a source line for the text that would appear on standard output."),
("A standard stream is a conventional channel through which a process communicates with its environment. Standard output can be shown in a terminal, captured by an integrated development environment or redirected by another process. The simple example is about this channel rather than about one particular window style.",
 "If a later learner sees no line in the expected pane, the cause might be a hidden or redirected output stream rather than a faulty string literal. That possibility should be investigated only after confirming that the correct source was run. This report itself has no observed stream capture to diagnose."),
("The normal end of a print call is often described as a newline. On a displayed page, that is usually represented by moving subsequent text to the next line. The visible greeting still has thirteen characters; the line boundary is a separate feature of the output operation.",
 "A learner can predict a two-call example without executing it: two default print calls should normally produce two lines in order. Replacing the first call's end value would alter this prediction. The point is not to implement such a variant here but to isolate where line layout comes from."),
("Manual counting should be reproducible. Index the visible message from one to thirteen: H, e, l, l, o, comma, space, W, o, r, l, d, exclamation mark. The sequence makes it possible to verify the total independently of a chart. The l appears three times; o appears twice; each other letter appears once.",
 "This count treats characters as displayed textual elements, not as raw file bytes. The delimiting quote marks and the implicit line ending are excluded. A figure can then display either category totals or individual-character frequencies without inventing an external dataset."),
("The source-to-output diagram is deliberately an abstraction. A Python implementation can have internal stages beyond the boxes shown, including parsing and the runtime's stream handling. Drawing fewer boxes does not assert that the internal process is identical on every computer; it shows only the relationships needed to explain this example.",
 "The distinction between a source-level error and a display-location issue follows from the pipeline. A malformed call may stop before an output request exists. A correct output request may instead be routed into a captured stream. Knowing where a problem could arise is more useful than memorising one error message."),
("An environment check should begin with the least speculative questions. Which Python version is selected? Is the expression written in a Python source context rather than a command shell? If a file is involved, is it the intended file? Only after those questions should a learner conclude that a missing greeting comes from incorrect print syntax.",
 "This is a proposed checklist, not a report of environment setup. Because the attached certificate does not specify an operating system or editor, no installation screenshot or institution-specific command is fabricated for this study."),
("An interactive prompt and a saved script offer different wrappers around the same instruction. In the interactive case the prompt shows readiness to receive input; the prompt characters are not part of the greeting. In the file case a command or editor action starts processing; that launcher is not part of the string value.",
 "When comparing two examples, remove the wrappers and examine the instruction and its expected result. This keeps the conceptual lesson stable even if one learner uses a terminal and another uses a notebook. It does not imply that either mode was used by the student during training."),
("Error messages are evidence only when they come from a genuine run in a documented environment. In a theory report, it is better to classify what could go wrong than to invent an exact traceback. An unmatched quote affects lexical interpretation; a mistyped function name affects name resolution; a changed space affects content but not validity.",
 "These categories suggest a correction order: first make the source valid, then ensure the requested operation exists, and finally compare the resulting message with the specification. That sequence avoids treating every unexpected screen as the same kind of failure."),
("A quotation delimiter determines where a literal begins and ends. If the opening mark has no suitable close, Python cannot obtain the intended string from the source. The correct response is to inspect the delimiters rather than to change the word Hello or add unrelated imports.",
 "It is also possible to write a valid string that itself contains quote characters, but that is a separate representation exercise. The reference line avoids this complexity by displaying only letters, comma, space and exclamation mark. Keeping the reference unambiguous makes it easier to distinguish error types."),
("A bracket check can be done without tools by marking each opening parenthesis and its matching close. In the one-call example, the sole pair surrounds one string argument. If an opening bracket remains unmatched at the end, the intended call is incomplete.",
 "This procedure should be applied to the source structure, not to arbitrary parentheses that might occur as characters inside a quoted message. The difference between syntax and data remains central: symbols inside a string can be displayed literally, whereas brackets around the argument list organise the call."),
("The visual similarity of print, Print and prnit makes them useful comparison cases. Python identifiers are case-sensitive, so a capital letter creates a different name. A transposed pair of letters likewise creates a different identifier. The report cannot guarantee one exact wording for every future error because the surrounding environment and Python version can differ.",
 "A learner should check names against the language reference or a known-good source line before altering the desired output. Spelling the name correctly enables the function to be found; it does not automatically verify that the message argument itself is exact."),
("Whitespace deserves two questions. Is a space part of the message value, or is it outside the quotation delimiters? A space inside the literal can change displayed text, while source spacing between tokens may serve readability or other grammar roles. The reference message has exactly one space after the comma.",
 "For a manual comparison, place two strings on separate lines and align positions rather than relying on overall appearance. Two neighbouring spaces can be hard to notice visually but make an exact equality check fail. The distinction matters even though the reader understands both sentences."),
("The one-string and two-string forms illustrate observational equivalence for a narrow purpose. With a default separator, both can display the same visible greeting. Yet they differ in how the space is produced. If the separator setting changes, only the multi-argument form is affected by that change.",
 "This contrast helps the reader avoid an oversimplified rule such as 'a space is always added by print'. The default separator acts between values; it does not insert extra spaces inside a single literal. The topic stays small while introducing a useful idea about output formatting."),
("The default separator is one space between arguments; it is not added before the first or after the last argument. If there is only one argument, changing the separator has no inter-argument boundary on which to act. With two arguments, an empty separator removes the join space and a dash makes the join visible.",
 "The comparison table should be read as a prediction under explicitly stated values. Its rows are not different runs of a program. A student who later performs the exercise could capture actual output and compare it with these predictions."),
("The end parameter is independent of the separator parameter. Separator text occurs between multiple positional values; ending text follows the completed output. In the reference case there is one argument, no inter-argument separator appears and the default ending starts a new line.",
 "This model helps answer a practical question: if the next prompt appears immediately after the greeting, which setting should be inspected? The ending behaviour is the relevant concept, not the spelling of World. The report does not claim a prompt was observed; it presents a diagnostic hypothesis."),
("An explicit newline inside a string is part of that string's value. The default ending of print is added after the argument has been displayed. Combining both would place more than one line boundary into the output scenario. The reference greeting uses only the normal ending and contains no explicit newline.",
 "On paper, a backslash-n escape is written as two source characters but denotes a line break in the resulting string. This difference between source representation and value is another reason not to infer visible output by counting raw symbols in a source line."),
("A variable can refer to a string value that was written earlier. The later print call uses that value; it does not display the variable's name unless the name itself is supplied as a literal. This gives a gentle transition from fixed messages to values that can be reused.",
 "For a one-time greeting, however, adding a variable does not make the displayed result more correct. It is an educational variation useful for explaining assignment and lookup, not a requirement or evidence of implementation. A later practical task could compare both forms under the same expected-output rule."),
("The reference characters are all in the basic English-letter and punctuation set that common encodings represent straightforwardly. Python 3's text model is broader: strings can hold many writing systems and symbols. The choice of a basic greeting limits the representation questions a first lesson needs to answer.",
 "A future international greeting might display correctly in one environment but require suitable fonts and encoding in another. That complexity does not alter the definition of print; it changes the conditions for representing and viewing a particular value. No internationalised output is claimed in this report."),
("In source code, changing the function identifier can alter which operation is requested. In the literal, changing uppercase letters alters the data requested for display. A case-sensitive language treats these as different levels of meaning even though both changes may be described casually as 'capitalisation errors'.",
 "A simple diagnosis asks which level changed. If the name is unknown, inspect the identifier; if the operation is valid but the greeting reads 'hello, world!', inspect the string value. This conceptual classification predicts the appropriate correction without inventing an actual error log."),
("Printing a string and returning a string differ in where information goes. A print call causes text to be sent to a stream for a reader or a capturing tool; it does not hand the greeting back as a reusable return value. That is why showing the right words on screen is not the same as giving another function a text result to process.",
 "An elementary thought experiment makes the distinction clearer. If another part of a hypothetical program needs the greeting as data, it should receive a value rather than scrape terminal output. This report neither constructs that program nor tests it; the contrast is included to prevent a frequent conceptual error about the meaning of output."),
("One can state the first program's result as a five-step proof rather than as a screenshot. The argument is a literal with value Hello, World!. The built-in print operation accepts that value. With one argument there is no separator to insert. The text is written to standard output. The default ending terminates the line.",
 "Each step has a condition that could change in a variant. More arguments make separator reasoning relevant; a changed literal makes the displayed text different; a changed ending changes layout. The manual trace is therefore reusable for several short exercises while remaining clearly distinct from an observed run."),
("A prediction should be precise enough to be falsifiable by a later exercise. 'A greeting appears' is too broad: it would not detect a missing comma, altered case or doubled space. 'The thirteen visible characters Hello, World! are written, followed by the default line ending' is more informative.",
 "At the same time, a prediction should not promise an exact terminal theme, font or prompt character, because those belong to the viewing environment rather than to the text value. The report separates the logical expected line from how a particular tool might display it."),
("For the category chart, manually sum the bar heights: ten letter characters plus two punctuation characters plus one space makes thirteen. This arithmetic is an internal check on the figure. The chart does not show milliseconds, pass rates or a sample of students; those values would be ungrounded here.",
 "A second valid view of the same string would count distinct letter frequencies: the letter ell occurs three times and o twice. Such a chart answers a different question from category composition. It should not be confused with a histogram of program executions or any measured runtime quantity."),
("A variant comparison needs a defined reference. This report chooses a comma, one space, title-style capitals and an exclamation mark. 'Hello World' and 'hello world' are understandable but are not exact matches. A much shorter 'Hi!' illustrates that length alone varies with content.",
 "The comparison is pedagogical, not a competition between greeting phrases. An instructor could choose a different reference without changing the underlying print rule. What matters is being explicit about the target string and distinguishing a valid alternate message from incorrect syntax."),
("The verification checklist can be treated as a small protocol: write a prediction first, identify the environment that would be used, perform one operation later, capture the observed text and compare it with the prediction. The present report covers only the prediction and design stages.",
 "A comparison that fails should record which property differs—spelling, case, spacing, punctuation or line layout—rather than simply call the whole program wrong. Such a record would be valuable in a practical follow-up, but no test log is manufactured for this theory-only submission."),
("The planned cases cover both valid statements with different text and a malformed statement that cannot produce the intended line. These should not be collapsed into one 'failure' category. A valid alternate greeting tests exact comparison; an unmatched quote tests the ability to recognise invalid source.",
 "To turn the plan into evidence, a learner would need to run each case in a named Python 3 environment and record actual observations. The current table deliberately reports predicted results only and does not include a column headed 'passed' or 'failed'."),
("A good diagnosis path asks questions in an order that narrows the cause. First confirm that the intended source was selected. Next determine whether the source can be interpreted. Then verify that the requested built-in name is correct. Finally inspect the exact message and the destination where output is shown.",
 "This is not a claim that every Python problem fits five boxes. Rather, it shows how the simple greeting can teach fault isolation. The diagram is a checklist for future work, not a screenshot of a debugging session."),
("A common mistake is to mistake visible output for the return value of the print function. Another is to treat quotation marks in source as characters displayed. A third is to assume that standard output must be a permanent file. Each misconception comes from collapsing two different roles into one.",
 "A short oral explanation can expose the difference: ask the learner what text is supplied, what operation receives it, where that operation sends it and what evidence would be needed to say it ran. Answering those questions is more informative than memorising the source line alone."),
("The learning application here is intentionally modest. A teacher could ask students to predict the reference line, explain why quotes disappear, compare two variants, and identify one source mistake from a supplied snippet. Those tasks measure conceptual understanding without requiring a fake industrial problem statement.",
 "The study can also serve as a baseline for later lessons. Once a learner understands literal output, replacing the literal with a variable or user input creates a genuine new topic. Nothing in this report asserts that such follow-up exercises were completed during the internship."),
("The design's limits should be visible to the reader rather than hidden at the end. There is no tested command, execution screenshot, captured standard output or measured performance in this document. Illustrations are derived from language rules or manual counting and are explicitly named as such.",
 "There is also an academic proportionality question: a greeting is only one line of source, while a fifty-five-page report is large. The extra pages explain surrounding fundamentals; they do not turn the greeting into a complex project. A supervisor should confirm that this subject is acceptable for the required internship submission."),
("A responsible future path separates stages. First practise the literal example. Next practise a variable-based form. Then accept a value from a user, define a function and record genuine test outcomes. Each stage adds new requirements that should be independently specified and checked.",
 "It would be misleading to label those stages as already completed merely because a diagram lists them. The diagram is a learning roadmap. If the department requests proof of actual practical work, the student should provide authentic source, screenshots and observations from work personally carried out."),
("The main result of the study is a set of distinctions: instruction versus displayed text, delimiting quotes versus string characters, an output stream versus a saved file, a predicted line versus an observed run, and an exact match versus a merely understandable greeting.",
 "These distinctions are transferable to later Python study. At the same time, the report remains honest about its scale. It documents a beginner topic using reasoned examples, diagrams and manually checkable charts rather than claiming that an application was implemented or that an employer assigned this exact exercise."),
]
